.Dd November 11, 2026
.Dt SELECT 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm select ,
.Nm newselect ,
.Nm pselect6 ,
.Nm pselect
.Nd wait for descriptor readiness
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/select.h
.Ft int
.Fn select "int nfds" "fd_set *restrict readfds" "fd_set *restrict writefds" "fd_set *restrict exceptfds" "struct timeval *restrict timeout"
.Ft int
.Fn pselect "int nfds" "fd_set *restrict readfds" "fd_set *restrict writefds" "fd_set *restrict exceptfds" "const struct timespec *restrict timeout" "const sigset_t *restrict sigmask"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
These functions wait for a descriptor to become ready, a timeout to
expire, or a signal to interrupt the wait.
The sets select descriptors from 0 through
.Fa nfds
minus one.
The value of
.Fa nfds
must not be negative; one past the end of the caller's descriptor table
is taken as that end, since no descriptor past it is open.
The table counts as at least as wide as a long's bits, 32 on i686 and 64
on x86-64, as Linux's always is.
A null set pointer selects no descriptors in that direction.
Each nonnull pointer must address the longs that hold the first
.Fa nfds
bits, and only those are read and written, as on Linux.
An
.Vt fd_set
holds
.Dv FD_SETSIZE ,
1024, bits; a program that raised its descriptor limit and selects a
higher descriptor passes an array of longs as wide as it needs.
.Pp
Use
.Fn FD_ZERO
to clear a set,
.Fn FD_SET
to add a descriptor,
.Fn FD_CLR
to remove one, and
.Fn FD_ISSET
to test membership.
On success, the sets contain only the ready descriptors.
Bits at or above
.Fa nfds
in the longs written are cleared.
.Bl -tag -width exceptfds
.It Fa readfds
Input is available, or the descriptor reports a hangup or error.
End of input counts as readable while the connection record is still
attached.
.It Fa writefds
Output can proceed, or the descriptor reports an error.
A hangup alone does not count as writable.
.It Fa exceptfds
Exceptional input is requested.
No descriptor currently reports this condition.
.El
.Pp
Regular files and directories report requested read and write readiness
immediately.
Pipes use their buffered data, free space, and peer lifetime.
Terminals use the line discipline's input state; pseudoterminals also
report peer hangup.
The console reports input only on a descriptor open for reading, and is
always writable, as a terminal is on Linux whatever the descriptor's mode;
a write through a read-only descriptor fails with
.Er EBADF .
.Pp
Datagram, packet, and raw ICMP sockets are readable when a packet is
queued and are always writable.
A listening stream is readable when a connection awaits acceptance.
A connected stream is readable with buffered input or a received FIN,
and writable in the established or close-wait state while its send
buffer has space.
A pending connection error counts as read and write readiness in the
requested sets; a closed stream counts as readable while its control
block remains attached, and reports no readiness once it is detached.
An ioctl-only socket reports an error and thus counts as ready in either
requested read or write set.
.Pp
Files under
.Pa /proc
report read readiness even at end of file.
Character devices, the framebuffer, and the mouse report requested
read and write readiness immediately.
Mouse readiness does not indicate that a packet is available.
.Ss Timeouts and signals
For
.Fn select ,
.Fa timeout
is a relative interval in seconds and microseconds.
For
.Fn pselect ,
it is in seconds and nanoseconds.
A null pointer waits indefinitely; a zero interval performs one scan
without waiting.
With no selected descriptors, a finite interval acts as a sleep.
A timeout ends at the first 1 ms timer tick at or after its end, measured from
where in the tick in progress the call is, so a timeout never expires
before the time asked, up to the limits below, and at most a tick after
it.
As in
.Xr poll 2 ,
the wait also lasts until
.Dv CLOCK_MONOTONIC
has advanced by the timeout after a lost tick.
.Pp
As on Linux, the raw calls write the time left back to a nonzero
.Fa timeout
as they return, whatever the result, by
.Dv CLOCK_MONOTONIC
against the end fixed at the call, and 0 once it has passed.
A process whose
.Xr personality 2
has
.Dv STICKY_TIMEOUTS
keeps its timeout as it gave it.
A signal that ends the wait with no handler run, as a stop and a continue,
restarts the call with the time left when it was interrupted.
The time spent stopped is added to the first deadline, as on Linux.
A handler, whatever its flags, makes it fail with
.Er EINTR .
When the time left cannot be written, the call fails with
.Er EINTR
in either case, and a failed write changes no other result, as on Linux.
.Pp
The raw
.Nm select
and
.Nm newselect
calls require nonnegative seconds and microseconds from 0 through 999999,
and take the timeout to the microsecond.
Musl normalizes excess microseconds before issuing the call.
.Pp
The raw
.Nm pselect6
call requires nonnegative seconds and nanoseconds from 0 through 999999999.
The tick of a deadline counts at most 2^40 seconds, about 34800 years, so
a timeout of 2^40 seconds or more reaches its tick only after that span.
.Pp
A non-null
.Fa sigmask
replaces the signal mask for the wait, atomically with its start.
When a signal ends the wait, its handler runs under
.Fa sigmask
and the previous mask comes back when the handler returns; otherwise the
previous mask comes back as the call returns.
A pending signal that is neither blocked nor ignored interrupts the call
if no selected descriptor is ready.
.Ss System call numbers
The old i386
.Nm select
call, number 82, takes a pointer in EBX to five 32-bit arguments:
the descriptor count, three set pointers, and the timeout pointer.
The
.Nm newselect
call, number 142, takes these arguments in registers.
Both use a timeval with 32-bit fields.
Musl's
.Fn select
uses number 142.
.Pp
The
.Nm pselect6
call, number 308, uses a timespec with 32-bit fields.
Musl's
.Fn pselect
uses this call.
Musl declares no functions named
.Fn newselect
or
.Fn pselect6 .
For positive seconds above the signed 32-bit maximum, both library functions
first try
.Nm pselect6_time64
(413), which the i686 kernel answers with
.Er ENOSYS ,
then fall back with clamped seconds.
The x86-64 kernel runs
.Nm pselect6_time64 ,
whose timespec has 64-bit fields, for a program that enters it by
.Ql int $0x80 ,
as it runs i386's other three calls, which
.Xr ia32 7
describes.
.Pp
On x86-64
.Nm select
(23) is the call i386 has as
.Nm _newselect ,
and
.Nm pselect6
is 270; their timeval and timespec have 64-bit fields.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
On success, these functions return the total number of set bits in the
returned sets, or zero on timeout.
A descriptor ready for both reading and writing counts twice.
On failure they return -1 and set
.Va errno .
The raw kernel calls return a negative error number.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/pollprobe.c
writes one byte to a pipe, selects its reader through system call 142
with no timeout, and checks for a return value of one and the reader's
bit in the returned set.
.Pa tests/musl/pselectmusl.c
blocks
.Dv SIGUSR1 ,
raises it, and calls
.Fn pselect
with a mask that blocks only
.Dv SIGUSR2 .
It requires -1 with
.Er EINTR ,
a handler that ran once with both signals blocked, and
.Dv SIGUSR1
alone blocked after the call.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width "[ENOMEM]"
.It Bq Er EBADF
A selected descriptor below
.Fa nfds
is not open, including one beyond the allocated descriptor table but
within a long's bits, or was opened with
.Dv O_PATH .
.It Bq Er EFAULT
A complete descriptor set cannot be read or written, the timeout cannot
be read, or the old call's argument array cannot be read.
.It Bq Er EINTR
No selected descriptor was ready and a signal that is neither blocked nor
ignored was pending.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
The descriptor count is negative, or the timeout is outside the accepted
range.
.It Bq Er ENOMEM
The kernel could not allocate the descriptor or wait arrays.
.It Bq Er ESRCH
There is no current process.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr pipe 2 ,
.Xr poll 2 ,
.Xr read 2 ,
.Xr socket 2 ,
.Xr write 2
